{
  "_readme": "For product/framework documentation (docs.python.org, docs.anthropic.com, MDN, ...). Docs are evergreen: a page isn't worse for being three years old if it still describes the current API, so recency decay is switched off entirely - the concrete case of 'use half-life or not' as a policy toggle. Peer-review and engagement don't apply to a docs page either (it has no venue and isn't measured by stars/HN points), so both are off too. Credibility here rests entirely on the domain tier - which is exactly why this mode needs its own tier reading: with every signal off, `score == tier base`, so the base policy's tier 3 for documentation (60) sits below the SUPPORT threshold (62) and NOTHING could ever pass. That is the bug the pilot found, and adding domains would not have fixed it. `domain_overrides` re-files official documentation and specifications as tier 1: for the question this mode asks - 'how does this API actually behave' - the vendor's own reference IS the primary source. Vendor engineering blogs are NOT promoted; they are commentary, not the reference. The only downward signal left is penalties.version_path, which catches an archived or superseded version of the same docs site.",
  "signals": {
    "recency_decay": false,
    "peer_review": false,
    "engagement": false
  },
